WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.890
Welcome to Tech Talk!

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.719
Today's topic is Open Liberty.

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:07.680
For those that follow this channel, you may
have seen my video on cloud-native Java and

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.877
how it helps enterprise developers extend
their investment in tools and skills into the future.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.850
Well, today we're joined by Harry Hoots, one
of the developers from Open Liberty.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.960
He's going to show us how they implemented
a fun demo that knits together lots of technologies

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.779
using Open Liberty and a host of other open
source projects.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:28.820
For those who haven't seen the game, the way
it works is that you have a play table, which

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.400
is with some planets and obstacles.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.640
The players use hand gestures that are detected
by a camera to navigate the game board.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.540
They have a fixed amount of time and they
have to avoid taking on damage on the way

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.180
to a destination planet.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.570
That's the game itself.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.440
This topology diagram shows the different
microservices that it uses on the back end,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.900
as well as a web server on the front end.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.110
With that intro out of the way, Harry, can
you explain how this Java EE project got started

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.309
and what were your goals?

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:57.309
Sure Dan!

00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:59.000
One little correction up front though.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.770
So instead of Java EE, it's now Jakarta EE. 

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:08.021
Java EE was Oracle's and they gave that over to the Eclipse Foundation around the end of 2017, beginning of 2018. 

00:01:08.021 --> 00:01:12.758
So now it's controlled by them as an open
source project to gives you Java EE functionality.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.820
Well, I've been around a little longer,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.470
so thank you for that historical note.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.470
You're welcome.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.470
All right.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.340
So if you want to know more about the game
service.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:27.303
So, first off, we have the game service itself,
which is basically the brainchild behind all this.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.320
We also --and it runs on Open Liberty --we've
also got the leaderboard service; again, an

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.030
Open Liberty service.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:39.079
We've got Prometheus and Grafana servers so
that we can scrape metrics and display them

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:40.400
on a dashboard.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.250
We also have a front-end UI where the user
walks up and types in their name and sees

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.060
their score, that kind of thing.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.899
So you divided this into a number of different
services and some other components.

00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:52.920
Why do you choose to do that?

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.920
How did it help you?

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:54.920
Yeah.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.680
So microservices architecture gives you a
lot of flexibility.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.110
If you think about your typical IoT or your
Java enterprise application, usually there's

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.760
a lot of legacy things in there and new things.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.500
And so you want to be able to tie all those
together.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.090
Microservices give you that flexibility to
take pieces, different architectures, different

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:16.110
languages, different hardware and put all
those together.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.270
And so that's why we did it.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:25.310
We wanted to highlight a MicroProfile-- excuse
me --microservices type of project that highlighted

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:30.010
Open Liberty and using MicroProfile and Jakarta
EE APIs.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.590
So MicroProfile, you mentioned that a few
times [that I mentioned in the prior video--

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.920
if you haven't seen it, be sure and check
it out].

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.959
Where was the payoff there for your team?

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:38.959
Yeah.

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:46.700
So, again, MicroProfile in Jakarta EE give
users a bunch of APIs developed for cloud-native

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.840
applications --streamlined, that sort of thing.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.920
It takes a lot of the time of programming
from the developer and gives them back time

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.340
to actually focus on the logic of the game.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:01.310
And speaking of the game, now that we've got
the big picture, could you kind of walk us

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.120
through from the user's point of view all
the way through these different services.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:06.120
What's going on?

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.120
Sure.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.989
So again, we've got web sockets that are bi-directional
servers on the hardware and the game service.

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:17.840
So when a user, for example, indicates a gesture
of left, a message is sent to the game service

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.450
and then it's routed over to the physical
rover.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:26.989
So on the physical rover, the Arduino board
takes that message, parses it, figures out,

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:28.510
"Oh, it's turn left".

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.750
And so then it turns the wheels and the appropriate
fashion.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.810
And so that loop repeats until the player
runs out of time in the game.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:42.689
And then throughout the gameplay, there is
an RFID sensor in the rover and then on the

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:45.680
different planets that you visit are RFID
cards.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.250
And then on the obstacles, there's NFC stickers
so that damage can be calculated.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.501
And so that information is fed back to the
game service and again goes to the UI to see

00:03:54.501 --> 00:03:56.290
your score and damage, etc..

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.720
And these two, I presume, communicate with
each other.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:03:59.720
That's correct.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.489
They communicate via REST APIs.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:04.549
Okay.

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:05.880
And then on this end, same deal?

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:06.880
That's the same thing.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.629
A REST API to the leaderboard service.

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:14.950
So by separating this out from your microservices
and Open Liberty components, you were able

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.189
to have your web developer focus just on that.

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:18.189
That's right.

00:04:18.189 --> 00:04:19.449
So that's the nice thing about microservices.

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:24.040
Typically, you would have an individual person
working on a particular service, or a small

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.470
team, depending if it's a larger type service.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:31.350
But, for example, one person worked on the
game service and the leaderboard service,

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:35.820
another worked on the UI, another worked on
the rover, another worked on the physical

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.090
map board that was hardware.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:41.850
Now, I gather you use Prometheus for pulling
statistics and showing some sort of things

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.169
that the developer is interested in?

00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:44.169
We do.

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:48.150
So Prometheus, the way we have an implemented
right now, is it's pulling JVM information

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.660
so we can see performance.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:54.160
We also have a timer there so that we can
see latency between the hand gestures and

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.160
the hardware.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.390
Well, speaking of latency, I imagine that
the whole goal of this was to deploy this

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.220
at a conference on a showroom floor.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.550
What did that deployment look like?

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.550
Yeah.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.970
So, typically at a conference, WiFi is going
to be terrible.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:10.259
So we usually run it on a local box on-prem.

00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:15.150
The beauty of it is though, we've also ran
it at conferences in the cloud and you can

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.229
mix and match those pieces too if you wanted
to.

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:20.490
That's the flexibility of the microservices
architecture.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.070
Run a few pieces in the cloud, run a few pieces
local.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:24.150
Just depends on your needs.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.039
That's really the hybrid cloud story, right?

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:27.026
That's correct.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:32.560
And so, to kind of wrap this up: What was the stack that you used? Because there's an awful lot of components.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.639
Can you kind of summarize what they were and
what they did?

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:37.635
Sure. So let's focus on the Open Liberty.

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:41.004
And so what Open Liberty offers is a full
open source stack.

00:05:41.004 --> 00:05:48.060
So we use OpenJ9 as the JVM. We use IBM Semeru Runtimes as the SDK.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.319
Open Library sits on top of that as our Java
runtime server.

00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:57.017
And then on top of that, Open Liberty implements
the specifications for both Jakarta EE and MicroProfile.

00:05:57.017 --> 00:06:01.020
But for the enterprise developers who are out there watching this, they're probably wondering: 

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:07.725
How do I take the lessons you learned from this game development and apply it to a business application that they might be doing?

00:06:07.735 --> 00:06:14.698
Sure. So, again, it fits really well-- this type
of thing for an enterprise application.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:20.550
This is an IoT implementation, but yet having
this microservice architecture and having

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.840
different components fits very well there.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.206
Okay. That's a great way of extending it.

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:27.090
That is for now a wrap.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.229
Since you stayed to the end of this video,
I bet you want to know more.

00:06:30.229 --> 00:06:34.759
So let me point you to a couple of great resources
related to cloud-native Java, microservices,

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:36.620
and of course Open Liberty.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.940
First, check out the GitHub repo with the
Space Rover mission.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.569
You can check out the source code and see
the services that Harry just covered.

00:06:44.569 --> 00:06:49.120
And if you're new to Open Liberty, you can
get started quickly with their hands-on guides.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.789
They cover how to build your first microservice
and then go deeper into cloud-native applications

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:57.131
featuring MicroProfile, Jakarta EE and more.

00:06:57.131 --> 00:06:59.154
Be sure to check it out in the links below.

00:06:59.154 --> 00:07:02.842
Before you leave, be sure and leave a Like and a Subscribe. 

00:07:02.842 --> 00:07:07.527
If there are other topics you'd like see on Tech Talk, be sure to also drop a comment below.

